#: manual/conformance/float.md:1
msgid "Float conformance and limits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/float.md:3
msgid "The contract separates exact integer encodings from portable numeric observations. Raw tests cover every classification, signed zero, exponent boundaries, NaN quiet bits, both signs, payload width limits and signaling-zero rejection. Rounding tests cover all five IEEE 754-2019 §5.9 directions, positive and negative ties, the predecessor of 0.5, negative zero, large integral values and infinities. Numeric tests use `x != x` for NaN and compare canonical integer output, never input NaN metadata."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/float.md:5
msgid "Integer conversion tests are table driven. For each binary format a generated table lists the exact rounded integer of every input in each of the five directions (computed offline with `fractions.Fraction`, or marked NaN, at least `2^64` or at most `-2^64`). The test converts every input with all sixteen functions in the checked-presence, checked-value and saturating modes, in all five directions through the `_with` forms and once through the default functions, and compares each result with the table. The inputs include `+0` and `-0`, `±0.5` and the other eighths below 9, halfway and quarter points next to every power of two `2^j` for `j = 0..69`, the sweep `2^k` and its neighbors one ulp away, the neighborhoods of `±2^31`, `2^32`, `±2^63` and `2^64` with fractional parts `0`, `1/8`, `1/4`, `1/2` and `3/4`, infinities, quiet and signaling NaNs with payloads, the smallest subnormal and the largest finite values, and 64 seeded random encodings. The default functions truncate; changing the default direction of any of them to `TowardPositive` fails the test (checked by mutation). For `Int64` and `UInt64` converted to binary32 and binary64, the table covers `2^k` and its neighbors for `k = 0..63`, halfway and nearby remainders for 24-bit and 53-bit significands, `2^64 - 1`, values near `2^64 - 2^39`, and `2^53 + 2^29 + 1`, where rounding through binary64 first gives the wrong binary32 result."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/float.md:7
msgid "Ordering tests cover both argument orders for minimum/maximum, signed-zero selection, raw totalOrder across signed qNaN/sNaN payloads, portable NaN canonicalization, and array and mutable-view sorting. The generated integer-key oracle compares all pairs in a boundary sample for both binary formats."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/float.md:9
msgid "Four-backend debug/release checks and exact transcript comparison follow the [CI contract](../testing.md). Local results cannot establish Linux GCC behavior; CI must confirm that job. Tests are regression evidence, not a proof of all IEEE 754 behavior. No Lean/mathlib formal proof is supplied."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/float.md:11
msgid "Exception flags, FMA, conversions, IEEE magnitude extrema, stable hashing, formatting, bigint and libm remain separate issues. Exact inexact-flag reporting for rounding waits for [issue #29](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/luna-utils/issues/29). Canonicalization is an application representation policy; it is not a promise to preserve floating-point payloads across a host round trip."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/float.md:13
#: manual/conformance/planned.md:5
msgid "Clause traceability"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance/float.md:15
msgid "IEEE Std 754-2019 for the host binary64 (`Double`) and binary32 (`Float`) types, through `Luna-Flow/luna-utils/float` and `Luna-Flow/luna-utils/float/binary32`, which expose the same names. Status is `conforming` when the operation exists and the cited evidence exercises it, `partial` when part of the clause is missing or the evidence is thin (the cell says which), `not implemented` and `not applicable` otherwise. `Level` is `required` for clauses the standard states with \"shall\" and `recommended` for those it states with \"should\". Evidence names a test file or a tool in the repository. The roadmap that orders the gaps is [issue #50](https://github.com/Luna-Flow/luna-utils/issues/50)."
msgstr ""

